REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nIn a humorous adaptation of Mark Twain\'s classic novel, continue the tale of the adventurous Tom Sawyer engaging in mischief and misadventures on the Mississippi River.\n### Response:\nTom Sawyer was not your average boy; he had an insatiable appetite for adventure and trouble alike. His curiosity often led him into situations that would make even the most seasoned sailor blush with embarrassment. But it wasn\'t just his penchant for mischief that made him so endearing - it was also his unwavering loyalty to his best friend Huckleberry Finn, who shared similar inclinations towards mayhem.\n\nOne sweltering summer day, as the sun beat down mercilessly upon the sleepy town of St. Petersburg, Missouri, Tom found himself bored out of his wits. He decided then and there that he needed something exciting to happen, or else he might go mad from ennui. And so, he concocted a plan: he would convince Huck to join him in stealing their own funerals.\n\n"Huck," he said, leaning close to ensure maximum', 'text2': "### Instruction:\nIn a humorous adaptation of Mark Twain's classic novel, continue the tale of the adventurous Tom Sawyer engaging in mischief and misadventures on the Mississippi River.\n### Response:\nTom Sawyer was known for his knack for getting into all sorts of trouble, much to the chagrin of his Aunt Polly. In our humorous retelling, we find young Tom embarking on yet another adventure along the great Mississippi River. \n\nOne sunny afternoon, as Tom lounged under a large oak tree by the riverbank, he noticed a peculiar sight: a group of ducks waddling around with hats perched atop their heads! Curiosity piqued, Tom decided to investigate further. As it turned out, these were no ordinary ducks - they were actually ducklings dressed up by some prankster!\n\nDetermined to uncover the mastermind behind this hilarious scheme, Tom enlisted the help of his loyal friend Huckleberry Finn. To