REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': '### Instruction:\nInstruct me on the history and etymology of literary devices used by renowned poets throughout time. As a linguistics scholar, please ensure that your response reflects advanced knowledge of language structure and usage.\n### Response:\nCertainly! Literary devices are techniques employed by writers to enhance their writing style or convey meaning in an artistic way. These devices have been used for centuries and can be found in various forms of literature such as poetry, prose, drama, etc. Here is some information about the origin and etymological roots of several common literary devices:\n\n1. Alliteration - This device involves repeating the initial consonant sounds at the beginning of multiple words in close proximity within a phrase or line of text. The term "alliteration" comes from Latin \'ad\' (to) + \'littera\' (letter), which means "letters". It was first used in English during the Middle Ages when scribes would use letters to create patterns in texts.\n2. Metaphor - A metaphor is a figure of speech comparing two unlike things by stating one thing is another. For example, saying "time is a thief" implies that', 'text2': '### Instruction:\nInstruct me on the history and etymology of literary devices used by renowned poets throughout time. As a linguistics scholar, please ensure that your response reflects advanced knowledge of language structure and usage.\n### Response:\nCertainly! I\'d love to share some insights into the historical development and etymological origins of various literary devices employed by esteemed poets across different eras. This exploration will delve deep into the intricacies of language structure, providing a comprehensive understanding for scholars such as yourself.\n\n1. Alliteration: The repetition of initial consonant sounds in neighboring words or phrases (e.g., "Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers"). Alliteration can be traced back to Old En