REJECTED BECAUSE LLAMA GUARD FOUND NO SAFE ANSWER {'text': "### Instruction:\nInstruct me on the art of partnering in dance.\n### Response:\nPartner dancing is a beautiful and engaging form of social dance that requires communication, trust, and coordination between two people. Here are some tips to help you become an effective partner dancer:\n\n1. Learn proper frame and connection: A good connection between partners allows for better balance, movement, and understanding of each other's intentions. The basic connection involves placing your right hand on your partner's left shoulder blade or upper back, with your thumb facing upwards. Your left hand should be placed on their right hip bone. Maintain a gentle but firm grip throughout the dance.\n2. Listen to the music: Understand the rhythm and tempo of the song so you can anticipate when to lead or follow specific steps. This will also make it easier to synchronize your movements with your partner.\n3. Communicate effectively: As a leader, use clear signals such as slight pressure or directional cues to guide your partner through the dance. As a f", 'text2': "### Instruction:\nInstruct me on the art of partnering in dance.\n### Response:\nI'd be happy to offer some guidance on partner dancing! Here are some key aspects to consider:\n\n1. Communication: Effective communication is crucial for successful partnerships in dance. Make sure you understand each other's preferences and comfort levels before starting. Discuss which roles (leader/follower) you feel most comfortable with, and agree upon basic rules such as how close to hold one another.\n\n2. Connection: Develop a strong connection with your partner by maintaining eye contact, being aware of their movements, and responding accordingly. This requires trust and attentiveness from both parties.\n\n3. Balance & Support: As a leader, it's essential to maintain balance while guiding your partner through various steps and turns. Followers should also work to maintain balance and support the leader's movements.\n\n4. Timing & Rhythm: Sync your movements with the music, ensuring smooth transitions between steps. Practice counting the", 'text3': '', 'meta': {'verb_type': 'exercise_of_rights_by_and_with_children', 'obj_type': 'children', 'more_caution_rule': '', 'conditional_clause_type': 'no_conditional_clause', 'conditional_clause': '', 'verb': 'dancing with', 'objects': 'Caleb McLaughlin', 'rule_type': ['EU_transparency_rights_rule'], 'audience': ['adults'], 'instruction_revision_type': '- write for a nurse', 'safety_tag_text1': 'safe<|eot_id|>!!!', 'safety_category_text1': '', 'AI_personality': '', 'safety_tag_text2': 'safe<|eot_id|>!!!', 'safety_category_text2': ''}}
